---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion, tableFor, column } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const cents = assertion('ninety-seven-cents-to-eighty-three')
const fell = assertion('the-county-fell-while-the-nation-rose')
const levelShare = assertion('the-level-does-not-separate-and-the-share-does')
const floor = assertion('twenty-eight-years-below-1989')

const series = assertion('thirty-one-years-of-poverty')
const noYear = assertion('not-one-year-separates-from-the-last')
const spans = assertion('spans-separate-and-the-ends-do-not')
const nation = assertion('indistinguishable-from-the-nation-until-2008')
const ratio = assertion('the-county-rate-against-the-national-rate')
const children = assertion('child-poverty-reached-three-in-ten')
const overlap = assertion('the-survey-and-the-model-overlap')

const povTable = tableFor('measure/allen-county-poverty-1989-2024.yml')
const years = povTable ? povTable.rows.map((r) => r[0]) : []
const rate = column(povTable, 'rate')
const lo = column(povTable, 'lo')
const hi = column(povTable, 'hi')
const usa = column(povTable, 'usa')

const incTable = tableFor('measure/allen-county-household-income-1989-2024.yml')
const incYears = incTable ? incTable.rows.map((r) => r[0]) : []
const share = column(incTable, 'pct_of_us')

const povSpec = line(
  years,
  [
    { name: 'Allen County', y: years.map((y) => rate.get(y) ?? null) },
    { name: 'Lower bound', y: years.map((y) => lo.get(y) ?? null) },
    { name: 'Upper bound', y: years.map((y) => hi.get(y) ?? null) },
    { name: 'United States', y: years.map((y) => usa.get(y) ?? null) },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Per cent below the poverty line', tickSuffix: '%' },
)
const shareSpec = line(
  incYears,
  [{ name: 'Allen County', y: incYears.map((y) => share.get(y) ?? null) }],
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of the national median household income', tickSuffix: '%', labelLast: true },
)
const fellSpec = bar(
  fell.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  fell.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Median household income, 2024 dollars', labelValues: true },
)
const ratioSpec = bar(
  ratio.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  ratio.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'County rate ÷ national rate', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="ninety-seven-cents-on-the-dollar">
  <p>
    The federal government has published an estimate of how many people in every county are poor,
    and what the median household there earns, once a year since 1989. It publishes something else
    beside each figure that most sources do not: a ninety per cent confidence interval. Reading
    thirty-one years of it for Allen County turns out to be as much a lesson in what a number can
    carry as in what happened here.
  </p>

  <h2>Ninety-seven cents, then eighty-three</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={cents} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="share"
    title="What the county's median household earns, against the country's"
    caption="Allen County's median household income as a percentage of the national median, 1989 to 2024. The ratio is computed on nominal dollars, where the price index cancels out."
    columns={['Year', 'Per cent of the national median']}
    rows={incTable ? incTable.rows.map((r) => [r[0], r[6]]) : []}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="share" set:html={JSON.stringify(shareSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={fell} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="fell"
    title="Median household income at each end of the record"
    caption="In the dollars of 2024, deflated with the all-items consumer price index. The county lost 4.3 per cent and the country gained 11.6."
    columns={['Geography and year', 'Median household income']}
    rows={fell.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `$${f.value.toLocaleString()}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="fell" set:html={JSON.stringify(fellSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={floor} />
  <p>
    A household at the middle of this county had less real income in 2024 than one at the middle in
    1989, and the country's had eleven per cent more. Twenty-eight of the thirty-one years measured
    stand below where the record starts. Only 1998 and 2019 stand above it, and 2019 is one of the
    two years the file's own arithmetic warns about.
  </p>

  <h2>What the interval will and will not let you say</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={levelShare} open />
  <p>
    This is the useful shape of the whole record. The county's real median income in 1989 and in
    2024 cannot be told apart — the two intervals overlap by three thousand dollars. Divide both by
    a national median that is measured on a sample forty thousand times larger, and its interval is
    a rounding error; the ratio inherits only the county's uncertainty and the two years separate
    cleanly. A comparison can be sharper than either of the numbers going into it.
  </p>

  <h2>Poverty, and thirty years of moves that are not moves</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={series} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="pov"
    title="The share of Allen County below the poverty line"
    caption="Thirty-one produced years with their ninety per cent bounds, against the national rate. The band is the point: the shape is real and no single step in it is."
    columns={['Year', 'Rate', 'Lower', 'Upper', 'United States']}
    rows={povTable ? povTable.rows.map((r) => [r[0], r[1], r[2], r[3], r[7]]) : []}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="pov" set:html={JSON.stringify(povSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={noYear} open />
  <p>
    Thirty adjacent pairs, and not one of them clears. The four-point jump into the recession year
    of 2009 has intervals that touch at exactly 16.7 and do not part. The four-point fall in 2013 —
    the largest single-year improvement in the record, the kind of number a press release is built
    on — has intervals overlapping by two tenths of a point. Neither is a fact about this county
    that the file can support.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={spans} open />
  <p>
    What the file resolves is two years and more. That is its own answer about its own resolution,
    and it is a far more useful thing to know than a general warning that estimates are uncertain.
    It also means the series cannot be read from its ends: 11.8 per cent in 1989 and 14.8 in 2024
    are the same number as far as this instrument is concerned, even though everything between them
    says otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Against the country</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={nation} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ratio} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ratio"
    title="Allen County's poverty rate divided by the nation's"
    caption="Below one, the county is less poor than the country; above it, poorer. The line is crossed once, in the middle of the 2000s, and not crossed back."
    columns={['Year', 'County ÷ nation']}
    rows={ratio.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(3)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ratio" set:html={JSON.stringify(ratioSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    For two decades this was a county a little better off than the country. It is now a county
    measurably worse off in seven separate years and never measurably better in any. The crossing
    is not a moment — no single year in this file is a moment — but it happens somewhere in the
    middle of the 2000s and it has not reversed in the eighteen years since.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={children} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={overlap} />
  <p>
    The five-year survey and the annual model disagree about 2023 by a point, and their intervals
    overlap, so the two may be set beside each other. They are not two witnesses. The model reads
    the survey as one of its inputs, and an instrument agreeing with something it was built out of
    is not corroboration — it is the same reading twice, at different grain.
  </p>
</Article>
